---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <Header />
  <main id="main">
    <section class="intro shell" aria-labelledby="intro-title">
      <div class="intro-content">
        <p class="eyebrow">
          <span class="tiny-star" aria-hidden="true">
            ✳
          </span>{' '}
          Hello, I'm Priyansh <span class="eyebrow-end">/ Software Engineer</span>
        </p>
        <h1 id="intro-title">
          I build software for <em>the complicated parts.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="intro-lede">
          I'm a software engineer at <strong>Thales</strong>. I work on backend and distributed
          systems — the kind where a seemingly small change can have consequences across services,
          data, and people.
        </p>
        <p class="intro-secondary">
          I like untangling that complexity, making sensible tradeoffs, and leaving systems easier
          to understand than I found them.
        </p>
        <div class="intro-links">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="#work">
            See what I've worked on <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="underline-link" href={site.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="underline-link" href={site.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            LinkedIn <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="intro-aside" aria-label="A note about my work">
        <div class="note-top">
          <span>IN MY OWN WORDS</span>
          <span class="note-asterisk" aria-hidden="true">
            ✳
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="note-body">
          <p>Most interesting problems don't announce themselves as architecture problems.</p>
          <p>
            They start as an edge case, a retry, a timing issue, or something that worked perfectly
            in a test and differently in production.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>That's the part I enjoy figuring out.</strong>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="note-bottom">
          <span>01 — WHAT KEEPS ME CURIOUS</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <div class="marquee-rule">
      <div class="shell factline">
        <span>BASED IN INDIA</span>
        <span class="fact-separator" aria-hidden="true">
          ·
        </span>
        <span>BACKEND SYSTEMS</span>
        <span class="fact-separator" aria-hidden="true">
          ·
        </span>
        <span>DISTRIBUTED PROCESSING</span>
        <span class="fact-separator" aria-hidden="true">
          ·
        </span>
        <span>AI & DEVELOPER TOOLING</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section
      class="work-section shell section-spacing"
      id="work"
      tabindex="-1"
      aria-labelledby="work-title"
    >
      <div class="section-intro-row">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">01 / SELECTED WORK</p>
          <h2 id="work-title">
            A few problems I've <span class="serif-accent">spent time on.</span>
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Not a list of everything I've touched. These are the kinds of engineering problems that
          have shaped how I think and work.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="story-list">
        <article class="story story-featured">
          <div class="story-detail">
            <div class="story-topline">
              <span>01 / DISTRIBUTED PROCESSING</span>
              <span>PROFESSIONAL WORK</span>
            </div>
            <h3>Making large-scale billing and reporting run reliably.</h3>
            <p>
              A reporting workflow is easy to describe: read events, calculate charges, generate a
              report. The interesting part is coordinating the work when processing is concurrent,
              data is large, and failures are inevitable.
            </p>
            <p>
              I worked on a multi-stage distributed processing framework, with particular attention
              to execution coordination, recovery, and performance validation.
            </p>
            <div class="story-tags">
              <span>Java</span>
              <span>Concurrency</span>
              <span>Cassandra</span>
              <span>Processing pipelines</span>
            </div>
            <a class="case-study-link" href="/work/distributed-billing/">
              Read the engineering case study <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
            </a>
            <div class="story-note">
              <span class="dot"></span> Public-safe architectural discussion; private implementation
              details omitted.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div
            class="story-visual pipeline-visual"
            aria-label="Illustrative stages of a data processing pipeline"
          >
            <div class="visual-label">
              A SYSTEM, IN STAGES <span>FIG. 01</span>
            </div>
            <div class="pipeline-layout">
              <div class="pipeline-stage">
                <span class="stage-num">01</span>
                <strong>Map</strong>
                <small>Distribute work</small>
              </div>
              <span class="pipeline-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                →
              </span>
              <div class="pipeline-stage">
                <span class="stage-num">02</span>
                <strong>Reduce</strong>
                <small>Aggregate</small>
              </div>
              <span class="pipeline-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                →
              </span>
              <div class="pipeline-stage">
                <span class="stage-num">03</span>
                <strong>Finalize</strong>
                <small>Build output</small>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="visual-bottom">
              <span>COORDINATION MATTERS BETWEEN THE BOXES.</span>
              <span>↗</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="story story-split">
          <div class="story-detail">
            <div class="story-topline">
              <span>02 / SYSTEM DESIGN</span>
              <span>PROFESSIONAL WORK</span>
            </div>
            <h3>Reworking the way an order scheduler thinks.</h3>
            <p>
              As scheduling logic grows, planning what should happen and actually executing it can
              become tightly coupled. I worked on separating those responsibilities and clarifying
              how different order types, rules, retries, and provider interactions should behave.
            </p>
            <div class="story-tags">
              <span>Architecture</span>
              <span>Orchestration</span>
              <span>Reliability</span>
            </div>
            <a class="case-study-link" href="/work/order-scheduler/">
              Read the engineering case study <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
            </a>
            <div class="story-note">
              <span class="dot"></span> Conceptual architecture; confidential implementation details
              omitted.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div
            class="story-visual split-visual"
            aria-label="Conceptual separation of decision and execution"
          >
            <div class="visual-label">
              SEPARATE THE CONCERNS <span>FIG. 02</span>
            </div>
            <div class="split-diagram">
              <div>
                <small>DECIDE</small>
                <strong>Planner</strong>
                <span>What should happen?</span>
              </div>
              <span class="vertical-separator" aria-hidden="true">
                ↔
              </span>
              <div>
                <small>DO</small>
                <strong>Executor</strong>
                <span>How does it happen?</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="visual-bottom">
              <span>ILLUSTRATIVE, NOT AN INTERNAL DESIGN DOCUMENT</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="story story-split">
          <div class="story-detail">
            <div class="story-topline">
              <span>03 / BUILDING FOR MYSELF</span>
              <span>DEVELOPER TOOLING</span>
            </div>
            <h3>Helping AI keep up with a real codebase.</h3>
            <p>
              AI tools are useful until the context gets messy. I've been experimenting with a
              persistent engineering knowledge workflow so I don't have to repeatedly explain the
              same architecture, conventions, and decisions across long-running development
              sessions.
            </p>
            <div class="story-tags">
              <span>AI tooling</span>
              <span>Knowledge capture</span>
              <span>Developer experience</span>
            </div>
            <div class="story-note">
              <span class="dot"></span> More details when a public, reproducible version is ready.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div
            class="story-visual notes-visual"
            aria-label="Conceptual engineering notes and their relationships"
          >
            <div class="visual-label">
              CONTEXT OVER TIME <span>FIG. 03</span>
            </div>
            <div class="note-stack">
              <div>
                <span>01</span> Architecture decisions <span>↗</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>02</span> Working conventions <span>↗</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>03</span> Lessons from changes <span>↗</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="visual-bottom">
              <span>KEEP WHAT MATTERS. RETRIEVE IT WHEN NEEDED.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="work-disclaimer">
        A note on these projects: some of my most substantial work has been on private systems. I
        share the engineering ideas and lessons, not proprietary code, configuration, or customer
        details.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" id="about" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="about-title">
      <div class="shell about-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">02 / A LITTLE ABOUT ME</p>
          <h2 id="about-title">
            Engineer by profession. <span class="serif-accent">Curious by default.</span>
          </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="about-text">
          <p>
            I started out enjoying the straightforward part of programming: solving a problem and
            getting the right answer. Working on real backend systems taught me to care just as much
            about everything surrounding that answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            What happens if the process dies halfway through? Who owns the state? What changes when
            the workload grows? Can someone else understand the design six months later?
          </p>
          <p>
            Those questions keep the work interesting for me. Outside my day job, I spend time on
            algorithms, engineering tools, and learning how good systems are put together.
          </p>
          <a class="underline-link" href={site.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            More about my experience on LinkedIn ↗
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      class="thinking-section shell section-spacing"
      id="thinking"
      tabindex="-1"
      aria-labelledby="thinking-title"
    >
      <div class="section-intro-row thinking-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">03 / THINGS I'VE LEARNED</p>
          <h2 id="thinking-title">
            The details <span class="serif-accent">are the work.</span>
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p>A few lessons I keep coming back to. None of them are glamorous. All of them matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson-list">
        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Failure paths deserve first-class attention.</h3>
          <p>
            Retries, duplicates, partial results, and recovery aren't edge decorations. They're part
            of the design.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>Simple to explain is a useful design constraint.</h3>
          <p>
            If a change needs too many exceptions to make sense, I usually need to revisit the
            boundaries.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>Measurements need context.</h3>
          <p>
            A benchmark is meaningful when you can explain the workload, environment, and what was
            actually measured.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closing-section" id="contact" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
      <div class="shell closing-layout">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">04 / SAY HELLO</p>
          <h2 id="contact-title">
            Have an interesting <span class="serif-accent">problem?</span>
          </h2>
          <p>
            I enjoy talking about backend engineering, distributed systems, tooling, and the
            tradeoffs behind building software. Feel free to reach out.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="closing-actions">
          <a
            class="button button-primary"
            href={site.linkedin}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Connect on LinkedIn ↗
          </a>
          <a class="underline-link" href={site.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Find me on GitHub ↗
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer credit="Designed and built with care." brandLabel="Back to homepage" />
</BaseLayout>
